description:
`internal/codegen/age/render_models_test.go` declares:

    // propertyWidths is every property type this backend carries, one row
    // per emitted Go type. Taken from the property table rather than written
    // out here, so a width the table gains is a row these sweeps gain.
    var propertyWidths = []graph.PropertyType{
        graph.TypeString, graph.TypeBool, graph.TypeInt32, graph.TypeFloat32,
        graph.TypeAnyPropertyValue, graph.ListOf(graph.TypeInt64, false),
        graph.TypeTimestamp,
    }

The comment says the rows come from the table. They are a hand-written list of seven, and the comment's consequence — 'a width the table gains is a row these sweeps gain' — does not hold: a width added to `typeMap.Property` gains no row here, and the two sweeps built on it (`TestZoneIsMarkedOnlyBesideTheInstant`, `TestEmittedHelpersAreClosedOverWhatTheyCall`) go on covering the same seven.

The same doc-comment sentence is repeated on `TestZoneIsMarkedOnlyBesideTheInstant` ('taken from the property table rather than written out here, so a width the table gains is a row this sweep gains'), so there are two copies of the false claim.

Both sweeps are about emission closure — that a batch carrying one width marks every helper it names. A width the table gains without a row is exactly the case they exist for, and it is the case they cannot see.

## Fix

Either derive the rows from the table, or say plainly that the list is hand-written and what keeps it honest.

There is now a deriving reader to reuse: `typeTableGoTypes` in `render_queries_test.go` (landed on gqlc-u039) reads every Go type text `typeMap` can name out of types.go's AST. These sweeps want `graph.PropertyType` values rather than Go type texts, so the reuse is not direct, but the AST-over-copy posture is.

Found during gqlc-u039's second-copy sweep.
